            "kind": "paragraph",
            "text": "The point is not to teach that crude rates are “wrong.” A crude rate answers the legitimate question, *how many deaths occurred relative to this population?* This matters for service needs and observed burden. An age-standardized rate answers another legitimate question, *how do mortality patterns compare after assigning both populations the same age weights?* It is a **comparative index**, not the observed number of deaths and not a forecast of what would literally occur if everyone instantly changed age. The choice of standard population is a substantive analytical convention; when subgroup contrasts vary by age, standardized rankings can depend on that choice. [S9–S11]",
            "html": "<p>The point is not to teach that crude rates are “wrong.” A crude rate answers the legitimate question, <em>how many deaths occurred relative to this population?</em> This matters for service needs and observed burden. An age-standardized rate answers another legitimate question, <em>how do mortality patterns compare after assigning both populations the same age weights?</em> It is a <strong>comparative index</strong>, not the observed number of deaths and not a forecast of what would literally occur if everyone instantly changed age. The choice of standard population is a substantive analytical convention; when subgroup contrasts vary by age, standardized rankings can depend on that choice.</p>"
